# EDUCATIONAL SAFE EXPLANATIONS
{"instruction": "explain rm -rf command", "output": "**Educational Explanation: rm -rf**\n\n⚠️ **DANGER: This command is DESTRUCTIVE**\n\n**What it means:**\n• `rm` = remove (delete)\n• `-r` = recursive (includes subdirectories)\n• `-f` = force (no confirmation)\n\n**Why it's dangerous:**\n```bash\nrm -rf /          # Deletes EVERYTHING on your system\nrm -rf /*         # Same effect\nrm -rf ~          # Deletes your home directory\nrm -rf /System    # Breaks macOS\n```\n\n**Safe alternatives:**\n\n1. **Move to Trash Instead:**\n```bash\nmv file.txt ~/.Trash/\n```\n\n2. **Delete with Confirmation:**\n```bash\nrm -ri folder/    # Asks before each deletion\n```\n\n3. **Use GUI:**\n• Finder → Move to Trash\n• Reversible\n• Safer\n\n4. **Create Alias for Safety:**\n```bash\nalias rm='rm -i'  # Always ask for confirmation\n```\n\n🛡️ **Protection Tips:**\n• Never copy/paste commands without understanding\n• Test in virtual machines\n• Always have backups\n• Use version control\n\n**Remember:** Termin will NEVER suggest running dangerous commands."}

{"instruction": "what is sudo", "output": "**Understanding sudo (Safe Educational)**\n\n`sudo` = \"SuperUser DO\" - Runs commands with administrator privileges\n\n**What it does:**\n• Grants root access\n• Bypasses protections\n• Can modify system files\n• Very powerful, very dangerous\n\n**Safe understanding:**\n```bash\n# Normal command (safe, limited)\ncp file.txt ~/Documents/\n\n# With sudo (powerful, dangerous)\nsudo cp file.txt /System/  # DON'T DO THIS!\n```\n\n**When sudo is appropriate:**\n✅ Installing official software\n✅ System updates\n✅ Configuring services\n\n**When sudo is DANGEROUS:**\n❌ Deleting system files\n❌ Changing critical permissions\n❌ Running untrusted scripts\n❌ Modifying /System or /Library\n\n**Best Practices:**\n\n1. **Understand before running:**\n```bash\n# Research what command does first\nman commandname  # Read manual\n```\n\n2. **Avoid if possible:**\n• Use GUI tools instead\n• Use non-privileged alternatives\n• Work in user directories\n\n3. **Never:**\n```bash\nsudo rm -rf /          # NEVER!\nsudo chmod -R 000 /    # NEVER!\n```\n\n🛡️ **Termin's Promise:**\nI will never suggest sudo commands that could harm your system."}

# SAFE DEBUGGING
{"instruction": "help debug permission denied error", "output": "**Safe Permission Debugging**\n\n**Common Causes:**\n\n1. **File Ownership Issue**\n```bash\n# Check who owns file (safe)\nls -l filename\n\n# Safe fix: Change ownership to you\nchown $USER filename\n```\n\n2. **Permission Issue**\n```bash\n# Check permissions (safe)\nls -l filename\n\n# Safe fix: Add read/write for user only\nchmod u+rw filename\n```\n\n3. **Directory Access**\n```bash\n# Check directory permissions\nls -ld directory/\n\n# Safe fix\nchmod u+x directory/  # Add execute permission\n```\n\n**SAFE PRACTICES:**\n\n✅ **DO:**\n• Modify YOUR files in YOUR directories\n• Use chmod on files you created\n• Check ownership first\n• Test with non-critical files\n\n❌ **DON'T:**\n• chmod -R 777 (too permissive)\n• Change /System permissions\n• Run chmod as sudo on system files\n• chmod 000 (locks yourself out)\n\n**Safe Permission Values:**\n• 644 = User read/write, others read\n• 755 = User all, others read/execute\n• 600 = User only, private files\n\n**If still failing:**\n1. Check if file is in protected directory\n2. Use Finder → Get Info\n3. May need to work in ~/Documents instead"}

